<section class="cm-infra" aria-label="Connected six-layer credential architecture" data-connected-infrastructure="">
  <h2>Evaluate the connections between shared capabilities</h2>
  <p>Sertifier documents W3C credentials, enterprise workflows and a CLR product. The useful distinction is therefore how the capabilities work together. CertifyMe presents six connected layers, with institution-reviewed skills and its own Job Engine supporting the workforce layer.</p>
  <p class="cm-infra-flow">Achievement → Credential → Standards → Verification → Skills → Learner Record → Workforce Intelligence → Opportunity</p>
  <ol>
    <li><span class="cm-stage" aria-hidden="true">01</span><h3>Presentation &amp; Access</h3><p>Design, credential pages, unique URLs, QR access and sharing make the achievement accessible. The visible record leads to its structured data; a QR code is access, not proof.</p></li>
    <li><span class="cm-stage" aria-hidden="true">02</span><h3>Standards &amp; Interoperability</h3><p><a href="/blog/why-institutions-should-embrace-open-badges-3-0-standards">Open Badges 3.0</a> and the <a href="/blog/Understanding-W3C-Verifiable-Credentials.html">W3C VC model</a> structure claims for compatible exchange. Confirm the implemented profile and receiver before relying on interoperability.</p></li>
    <li><span class="cm-stage" aria-hidden="true">03</span><h3>Verification &amp; Trust</h3><p><a href="/certificate-verification">Verification</a> examines issuer authority, proof integrity and applicable status. That record supplies achievement context for skill review; valid proof alone does not establish competence or independent learner identity.</p></li>
    <li><span class="cm-stage" aria-hidden="true">04</span><h3>Skill Taxonomy Alignment</h3><p>Academic review relates outcomes and evidence to <a href="/skills-taxonomy-mapping">ESCO, O*NET or institutional skill frameworks</a>. Those reviewed relationships can travel with the achievement into a learner record.</p></li>
    <li><span class="cm-stage" aria-hidden="true">05</span><h3>Comprehensive Learner Record</h3><p>A <a href="/comprehensive-learner-record">CLR</a> connects achievements, skills and experiences with their provenance. It complements the transcript and supplies learning context for careers teams, not a replacement SIS.</p></li>
    <li><span class="cm-stage" aria-hidden="true">06</span><h3>Workforce Intelligence &amp; Opportunity</h3><p><a href="/workforce-intelligence">Workforce Intelligence</a> relates reviewed skills and learner-record context to occupations, jobs and employers through the Job Engine. Opportunity insight informs decisions; it does not guarantee employment.</p></li>
  </ol>
  <p>Use a multi-department pilot to examine issuance, standards profiles, record aggregation, residency terms and workforce-data scope. Compare the contracted implementation rather than assuming either product uniquely supplies the whole journey.</p>
